window.REVIEW_DETAILS["ny_main-5d71b90ece963b95"]={"key":"ny_main-5d71b90ece963b95","group":"ny_main","uid":"5d71b90ece963b95","rank":80,"title":"yewbank cinderhall phased pension access response","occupation":"personal_financial_advisors","archetype":"authored_doc","subbatch":"corpus_main","prompt":"You are a Personal Financial Adviser at Yewbank Provisions, taking over Priya Kalinowski’s defective pension-access response before Hana Tanaka decides this week whether it can be issued to Nils Garrido, a 56-year-old Cinderhall Clinic owner resident in England. Meera Garrido’s 28 March email requested “£60,000 gross immediately,” while Nils’s direct interview on 11 April recorded that he needs £22,500 after income tax by 30 September 2025 and a further £22,500 by 31 May 2026, with no continuing income requirement. Nils uses “phased access” to mean those two payment dates; Priya’s draft uses the same term for repeated uncrystallised-funds pension lump sums, so Hana will not accept it until the intended meaning is pinned down. The case record shows a £240,000 uncrystallised pot, no previous use of the lump sum allowance, taxable non-pension income of £42,000 in each of 2025/26 and 2026/27, and intended gross money-purchase contributions of £16,000 after the proposed access in each tax year. Genevieve Wickramasinghe’s 2 April handover called the arrangement ordinary defined contribution benefits with no guarantees, but the provider status dated 8 April says benefit classification, access availability and charges remain pending. Priya also says a Pension Wise appointment is compulsory, whereas Genevieve describes it as merely suggested; the response must draw a defensible boundary between what must happen and what should happen. Prepare an executive-ready corrected customer case response for Hana’s approval, comparing the realistic access alternatives across the two tax years, recommending the defensible option, stating projected outcomes, and identifying the next action if the unresolved evidence prevents completion. Apply England rules as at 14 April 2025. Treat the tax figures as a frozen snapshot, assume no investment growth or inflation and unchanged 2026/27 income-tax bands, and do not invent returns, provider charges or product features. Use plain English for a senior reader who does not share Priya’s investment terminology, and keep the document to two pages. Write it into the top level of the working directory using exactly the filename `cinderhall_pension_case_response.docx`.\n\n---\n\nWrite your finished work to the **top level of the current working directory**, using exactly these filenames:\n\n- `cinderhall_pension_case_response.docx` — Executive-ready corrected pension-access case response for peer approval. (Place the file at the top level of the working directory.; Use exactly the specified filename.; Limit the rendered document to two pages.; Use England pension and income-tax rules as at 14 April 2025.; Do not invent investment returns, provider charges or provider-specific product features.)\n\nNothing outside those filenames is collected. A correct file written anywhere else, or under any other name, scores zero.\n","inputs":[],"gold":[{"name":"package/private/gold/cinderhall_pension_case_response.docx","bytes":42068,"extension":".docx","url":"assets/95a2ae1b1e2ae19730bcac2175061f9b9807c708251f88a0b623169d4ab4822c.docx","sha256":"95a2ae1b1e2ae19730bcac2175061f9b9807c708251f88a0b623169d4ab4822c","preview":{"kind":"text","text":"Cinderhall Clinic pension access\nCorrected customer case response for Hana Tanaka's approval\nCustomer: Nils Garrido (56)  |  Legal and tax position: England, as at 14 April 2025\nCurrent objective and terminology\nNils's direct interview of 11 April is the current instruction: £22,500 after income tax by 30 September 2025 and a further £22,500 by 31 May 2026, with no continuing-income requirement. Meera's 28 March email sought £60,000 gross immediately; it is older, differs in amount basis and timing, and must not displace Nils's instruction.\nFor this case, 'phased access' means two staged partial crystallisations with a payment on each of those dates. It does not mean repeated uncrystallised-funds pension lump sums (UFPLS), which take a mixed tax-free and taxable payment directly from uncrystallised funds.\nConditional recommendation\nApprove only in principle: subject to Nils's written confirmation and provider verification, use two partial crystallisations of £90,000. Take a £22,500 pension commencement lump sum (PCLS) each time, move the other £67,500 to drawdown, and take no taxable drawdown income. This is the defensible route because it meets the two net cash dates without taxable pension income or an MPAA trigger under the model.\nEvidence and model boundary\nKNOWN CASE FACTS\nFROZEN MODELLING ASSUMPTIONS\nEVIDENCE STILL REQUIRED\nEngland resident, age 56; £240,000 uncrystallised pot; £42,000 other taxable income in each tax year; £16,000 planned money-purchase contributions after access each year; no earlier lump-sum allowance use recorded.\nNo investment growth or inflation. England 2026/27 tax bands remain at 2025/26 values. Tax is final annual liability. Unverified provider charges are excluded.\nProvider status of 8 April leaves benefit classification, access availability and charges pending. Do not treat the 2 April handover's ordinary-DC/no-guarantees description as confirmation; obtain benefit type, access method, safeguards and charges.\nRecommended payment model\nThe table assumes the provider can make the requested access available and that no growth or charges arise before either payment.\nTax year / latest date\nCrystallise\nTax-free PCLS paid\nTaxable drawdown paid\nMoved to drawdown\n2025/26\n30 Sep 2025\n£90,000\n£22,500\n£0\n£67,500\n2026/27\n31 May 2026\n£90,000\n£22,500\n£0\n£67,500\nTotal\n£180,000\n£45,000\n£0\n£135,000\nThis delivers £22,500 by 30 September 2025 and £22,500 by 31 May 2026: £45,000 total customer cash. After both transactions, £180,000 is crystallised, comprising £45,000 paid as lump sums and £135,000 moved to drawdown; £60,000 remains uncrystallised. The £135,000 is not cash paid to Nils.\nLump-sum allowance: £268,275 less £45,000 used leaves £223,275, subject to confirmation that no allowance has previously been used. Taking the PCLS uses future tax-free lump-sum entitlement.\nAccess and contributions: at age 56, Nils is normally above the normal minimum pension age of 55 on 14 April 2025. That does not replace verification of any scheme-specific protected age or benefit restriction. PCLS-only access with no taxable drawdown does not itself trigger the money purchase annual allowance (MPAA), so the planned £16,000 contribution is not constrained by the £10,000 MPAA on this basis.\n\n\nTaxable-access comparison\nThe alternatives below use the frozen England tax assumption. They show final income-tax liability attributable to pension access, not an initial provider deduction.\nRoute\nFinal tax and customer cash\nContribution effect\nDecision relevance\nOne £60,000 UFPLS\n2025/26\n£15,000 tax-free + £45,000 taxable. Incremental tax £16,346; final net pension cash £43,654.\nTriggers the £10,000 MPAA. Planned £16,000 contribution is £6,000 above it.\nOlder gross/immediate request; lower final cash than the current net objective and no staged timetable.\nTwo £30,000 UFPLS\none per tax year\nEach: £7,500 tax-free + £22,500 taxable; tax £7,346; final net £22,654. Total tax £14,692; net £45,308.\nAlso triggers the £10,000 MPAA; the planned £16,000 is £6,000 above it in each post-access year.\nBetter tax result than one UFPLS, but introduces taxable pension income and an MPAA problem.\nRecommended: two partial crystallisations, PCLS only\n£22,500 on each date; £45,000 total tax-free cash. No taxable drawdown is taken.\nPCLS only, with no taxable drawdown, does not itself trigger the MPAA.\nMeets the two stated dates and net objective, conditional on provider and customer confirmation.\nThe two-period UFPLS result is £1,654 better than one £60,000 UFPLS because income-tax bands reset between tax years. The recommended PCLS-only route takes no taxable pension income, so it does not depend on that tax-band reset. A provider may initially withhold tax under an emergency code; do not promise an actual provider payment amount without its illustration.\nPractical consequences\n• The alternatives are not interchangeable: taking a PCLS consumes lump-sum allowance and moves the remaining crystallised funds into drawdown; taking UFPLS removes both the tax-free and taxable parts from the uncrystallised pot.\n• Only £45,000 is paid out on the recommendation. Cash removed from the pension loses the pension wrapper's tax treatment and protection. No investment return, inflation figure, charge or product feature has been assumed.\n• Taxable UFPLS would trigger the £10,000 MPAA. The intended £16,000 post-access money-purchase contribution is £6,000 above that limit in each tax year; PCLS alone and no taxable drawdown does not itself cause that result.\nPension Wise and approval controls\nBefore flexible access, the provider must apply the applicable stronger-nudge process unless an exemption applies: Pension Wise guidance must be received, or the customer must expressly opt out after the required process. Nils is not compelled to attend an appointment. Using the guidance is advisable, but it is not a mandatory customer appointment.\nIf either the customer instruction or provider evidence cannot be confirmed, no application should be submitted. The case owner must first obtain Nils's written confirmation of the two net amounts and dates. The provider must then confirm benefit type, the available access method, safeguards or guarantees, and all charges, and provide an illustration. Return the case to Hana for approval only after those confirmations are on file.\nScope\nThis recommendation is limited to the stated two-payment objective; it is not a complete assessment of sustainable retirement income. Any wider retirement advice still needs the customer's cash-flow needs, other pensions and assets, debts, health and dependants, investment preferences and tax position.","truncated":false,"note":"DOCX 正文与表格单元格文字提取；不还原分页、字体与版式。","embedded_image_count":0,"embedded_images":[]}}],"rubric":[{"rubric_item_id":"R01","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The required Word document exists at the working-directory top level under the exact filename `cinderhall_pension_case_response.docx` and opens successfully.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":true,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Submitted file path, extension and document integrity.","grader_hint":"Confirm the exact filename and top-level location, then open or parse the DOCX to ensure it is not corrupt."},{"rubric_item_id":"R02","check":"geometry","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The rendered response occupies no more than two pages.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Rendered DOCX page count.","grader_hint":"Render with a standard office suite and count actual pages; do not infer page count from word count."},{"rubric_item_id":"R03","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The response identifies England as the applicable jurisdiction and 14 April 2025 as the legal and tax position date.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"An explicit jurisdiction and as-of statement in the document.","grader_hint":"Accept equivalent wording only if both England and the exact as-of date are unambiguous."},{"rubric_item_id":"R04","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Before relying on the phrase “phased access,” the response defines it for this case as staged partial crystallisation and payment on two dates, and distinguishes that meaning from repeated UFPLS withdrawals.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"The first substantive use or an immediately preceding definition of the term.","grader_hint":"The reader must not be left to infer which of the two conflicting meanings applies. Merely expanding an acronym does not satisfy this item."},{"rubric_item_id":"R05","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The response treats the customer’s direct 11 April instruction—two after-tax payments of £22,500—as the current objective, rather than silently adopting the spouse’s older request for £60,000 gross.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Statement of the governing objective and treatment of the conflicting dated request.","grader_hint":"Look for explicit reconciliation of source, date, gross-versus-net basis and payment timing. The older request may be compared but must not displace the direct customer instruction."},{"rubric_item_id":"R06","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The response identifies benefit classification, access availability and provider charges as unresolved, and does not treat the earlier handover description as verified provider evidence.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Uncertainty or evidence-status discussion.","grader_hint":"Credit requires recognition that the later provider status leaves these matters pending. A generic disclaimer without identifying the unresolved items is insufficient."},{"rubric_item_id":"R07","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The response makes a clear conditional recommendation for two partial crystallisations taking pension commencement lump sums only, rather than recommending taxable UFPLS withdrawals as the preferred route.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Recommendation statement and its stated condition.","grader_hint":"The recommendation should be identifiable without reconstructing it from the comparison and should be conditional on provider verification and customer confirmation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R08","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The recommended projection shows that crystallising £90,000 for each instalment supports a £22,500 tax-free lump sum each time, producing £45,000 total customer cash under the stated assumptions.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Projected amounts for each date and the combined outcome.","grader_hint":"Check the 25% relationship for both instalments and the £45,000 total. Equivalent presentation is acceptable if all relationships are clear."},{"rubric_item_id":"R09","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"After both recommended partial crystallisations, the stated pension position reconciles to £180,000 crystallised, comprising £45,000 paid as lump sums and £135,000 moved to drawdown, with £60,000 remaining uncrystallised, assuming no growth or charges.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Post-transaction pension balances.","grader_hint":"Verify that all components reconcile to the original £240,000 pot and that the £135,000 drawdown balance is not presented as cash paid to the customer."},{"rubric_item_id":"R10","check":"value_tolerance","category":"deterministic","criterion":"For the earlier single £60,000 UFPLS alternative in 2025/26, the response projects £16,346 incremental income tax and £43,654 final net pension cash, each within ±£2.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Single-withdrawal comparison figures.","grader_hint":"Recalculate using a 25% tax-free component, £45,000 taxable pension income, £42,000 other taxable income, a £12,570 personal allowance and the England 2025/26 basic-rate band. Judge final liability rather than temporary provider withholding."},{"rubric_item_id":"R11","check":"value_tolerance","category":"deterministic","criterion":"For two £30,000 UFPLS withdrawals in separate tax years, the response projects incremental income tax of £7,346 and net cash of £22,654 per year, or £14,692 tax and £45,308 net in total, each within ±£2.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Split-UFPLS comparison figures for both periods and in total.","grader_hint":"Each withdrawal is 25% tax-free and 75% taxable. Apply the stated £42,000 other income and frozen England bands independently in each tax year."},{"rubric_item_id":"R12","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The response explains that resetting the income-tax bands between tax years improves the split-UFPLS final net outcome by £1,654 ±£2 relative to taking £60,000 in one year, while noting that the recommended lump-sum-only route does not depend on that tax-band reset.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Period-over-period explanation tied to the projected totals.","grader_hint":"Look for both the numerical difference and a concise explanation of why the periods matter. A table of figures without explaining the material change is insufficient."},{"rubric_item_id":"R13","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Known case facts, modelling assumptions and unresolved evidence are visibly distinguished, including no growth, no inflation, unchanged 2026/27 bands and exclusion of unverified charges.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Labels or prose that separates facts, assumptions and matters awaiting confirmation.","grader_hint":"No particular layout is required. The distinction must be clear enough that Hana can tell which projected outcomes would change if new evidence arrives."},{"rubric_item_id":"R14","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The response states the £268,275 lump sum allowance, applies £45,000 of it to the recommended payments and shows £223,275 remaining, subject to confirmation that no allowance has previously been used.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Lump sum allowance treatment and arithmetic.","grader_hint":"Accept plain-English terminology, but the cap, usage and remaining allowance must reconcile. The available allowance must not be confused with the pot’s maximum 25% tax-free amount."},{"rubric_item_id":"R15","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The response states that an UFPLS taxable-access route triggers the £10,000 money purchase annual allowance, identifies the planned £16,000 post-access contribution as £6,000 above it, and explains that taking pension commencement lump sums without taxable drawdown does not itself trigger the MPAA.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Contribution and MPAA comparison for the alternatives.","grader_hint":"Credit requires the trigger distinction as well as the £6,000 comparison. Do not credit a claim that crystallisation or a tax-free lump sum alone automatically triggers the MPAA."},{"rubric_item_id":"R16","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The response correctly identifies that a 56-year-old may normally access pension benefits on 14 April 2025 because the normal minimum pension age is then 55, while leaving any scheme-specific protected-age or benefit restriction subject to verification.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Eligibility discussion tied to age, date and provider evidence.","grader_hint":"The response must not apply the scheduled age-57 threshold prematurely or use age eligibility to override pending scheme-specific checks."},{"rubric_item_id":"R17","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The response draws the Pension Wise must/should boundary correctly: the provider must apply the applicable stronger-nudge process before flexible access unless an exemption applies, but the customer is not compelled to attend because guidance may be received or expressly opted out of; using the guidance is presented as advisable rather than mandatory.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Explanation of the provider’s process obligation and the customer’s choice.","grader_hint":"Reject either extreme that an appointment is always legally compulsory for the customer or that the provider may simply omit the referral and opt-out process."},{"rubric_item_id":"R18","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The UFPLS comparison distinguishes projected final income-tax liability from any initial emergency-code withholding and does not promise the amount a provider will pay before receiving a provider illustration.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Tax-payment timing caveat attached to the UFPLS figures.","grader_hint":"Look for a concise distinction between final tax cost and initial cash withholding. Detailed reclaim instructions are not required."},{"rubric_item_id":"R19","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The recommended two-payment route delivers £22,500 by 30 September 2025 and £22,500 by 31 May 2026 under the model, matching the current £45,000 after-tax objective without treating £60,000 gross as the target.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Recommended payment schedule, amounts and target comparison.","grader_hint":"Check amount basis and dates together. A recommendation that produces the right total but misses either required date does not satisfy the criterion."},{"rubric_item_id":"R20","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The comparison identifies decision-relevant tradeoffs beyond headline tax, including loss of future tax-free entitlement when allowance is used, movement of funds from the uncrystallised pot into drawdown or cash, loss of pension-wrapper protection for withdrawn cash, and the effect of taxable access on future contributions.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Concise comparison of consequences for the customer.","grader_hint":"Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable. The response need not become a general retirement tutorial, but it must show why the alternatives are not interchangeable."},{"rubric_item_id":"R21","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"If the provider evidence or customer instruction cannot be confirmed, the response directs that no application be submitted and gives an executable next action: obtain the customer’s written confirmation of amount and dates and obtain provider confirmation of benefit type, access method, safeguards and charges before returning the case for approval.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Fallback instruction, information requests, owner or recipient and sequencing.","grader_hint":"The fallback must be operational rather than a vague statement to investigate further. It should prevent implementation on incomplete evidence."},{"rubric_item_id":"R22","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The response limits its recommendation to the stated two-payment objective and does not present the projection as a complete assessment of sustainable retirement income, while briefly flagging any material suitability information still needed before broader advice.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Scope statement and treatment of the absence of an ongoing-income requirement.","grader_hint":"Credit a concise boundary. Do not require a full fact-find or generic list of every retirement risk."},{"rubric_item_id":"R23","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document is executive-ready: its recommendation, rationale, projections, conditions and fallback can be understood without specialist terminology or a follow-up question, and amounts and conclusions agree throughout.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"The document as a whole.","grader_hint":"Review as Hana would. Penalize unexplained acronyms, buried conclusions, contradictory totals, tutorial-like digressions or uncertainty phrased so vaguely that no approval decision can be made."},{"rubric_item_id":"P01","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The response invents or presents as fact a provider charge, investment return, inflation figure, provider-specific feature or product/fund recommendation that is not supported by the case.","weight":-6.0,"polarity":"penalty","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Unsupported numerical or product-specific assertions in the submitted document.","grader_hint":"Apply once if the document fills an unresolved field with fabricated precision or expands into an unsupported product recommendation. Do not apply merely because it clearly labels a hypothetical sensitivity as hypothetical."}],"spec":{"professional_context":"A personal financial adviser is correcting a retirement-access case response for senior approval before it is issued to a customer. The response must reconcile conflicting dated evidence, model the available routes and remain conditional where provider evidence is incomplete.","difficulty_sources":["Reconciling a later direct customer instruction with an older stakeholder request that uses a different amount basis.","Modelling taxable and tax-free pension-access alternatives across two tax years.","Separating verified facts, frozen assumptions and unresolved provider evidence.","Defining ambiguous pension-access terminology for a non-specialist decision maker.","Drawing the statutory must/should boundary for Pension Wise and the stronger-nudge process."],"authoring_plan":{"reference_material":{"files":[],"instruction":"Do not create reference files. All case-specific facts are contained in the request; the discriminating content comes from UK retirement-planning knowledge as at the stated date."},"evidence_conflicts":["The older spouse email requests £60,000 gross immediately, while the later direct customer interview requests two payments of £22,500 after income tax on specified dates. The gold should use the later direct customer instruction as the current objective and seek written confirmation before implementation.","An earlier handover characterises the pension as ordinary defined contribution benefits without guarantees, while the later provider status leaves benefit classification, access availability and charges pending. The gold must not treat the earlier description as verified.","The phrase “phased access” has two meanings in the case. The gold must define it as two staged partial crystallisations and distinguish this from repeated UFPLS withdrawals.","Conflicting statements about Pension Wise must be resolved by separating the provider’s stronger-nudge process obligation from the customer’s ability to receive guidance or opt out."],"calculation_rules":{"tax_basis":"Use England 2025/26 personal allowance of £12,570, basic-rate band of £37,700, 20% basic rate and 40% higher rate. Apply the same figures to 2026/27 under the supplied frozen assumption. Pension income does not attract National Insurance.","baseline_income":"With £42,000 non-pension taxable income, baseline income tax is £5,886 in each period.","single_ufpls":"A £60,000 UFPLS has £15,000 tax-free and £45,000 taxable. Total income tax is £22,232, so incremental tax attributable to the pension is £16,346 and final net pension cash is £43,654.","split_ufpls":"Each £30,000 UFPLS has £7,500 tax-free and £22,500 taxable. Total income tax in each period is £13,232, so incremental tax is £7,346 and net pension cash is £22,654. Across two periods, incremental tax is £14,692 and net cash is £45,308.","period_difference":"The two-period UFPLS result exceeds the one-period £60,000 UFPLS net result by £1,654 because the income-tax bands reset between periods.","recommended_route":"To pay £22,500 as a pension commencement lump sum, partially crystallise £90,000 and move the remaining £67,500 to flexi-access drawdown without taking taxable drawdown income. Repeat for the second instalment. Under no-growth and no-charge assumptions, £45,000 is paid tax-free, £135,000 sits in drawdown and £60,000 remains uncrystallised.","lump_sum_allowance":"Use the £268,275 lump sum allowance as at the stated date. Applying £45,000 leaves £223,275, subject to confirmation that the full allowance is available.","mpaa":"The money purchase annual allowance is £10,000. An UFPLS triggers it; £16,000 of planned post-trigger money-purchase contributions is £6,000 over that limit. Taking only a pension commencement lump sum and no taxable drawdown income does not trigger it."},"professional_rules":["Age 56 is above the normal minimum pension age of 55 on 14 April 2025, but age eligibility does not replace verification of scheme restrictions, protected rights or safeguarded benefits.","The provider should not process flexible access until the applicable stronger-nudge requirement has been met through guidance or a valid opt-out, subject to exemptions. The customer need not be told that attendance is invariably compulsory.","UFPLS figures should be described as final-liability projections. Initial emergency-tax withholding can differ, so an actual provider payment should not be promised without an illustration.","The recommendation should remain conditional until the customer confirms the current objective in writing and the provider confirms benefit type, access routes, safeguards and charges.","The response should distinguish verified facts, frozen assumptions and unresolved evidence, and should not fabricate missing provider or investment information.","The recommendation is limited to the two requested payments. It should not imply that a full retirement-income, investment, estate-planning or long-term sustainability assessment has been completed."],"intended_verdict":"Recommend two staged partial crystallisations taking pension commencement lump sums only, one for each £22,500 payment, because this meets the current after-tax objective without taxable pension income or an MPAA trigger under the frozen assumptions. Do not authorise implementation until the direct customer instruction and provider benefit details are confirmed.","gold_validation":["Use an offline calculation script to verify every tax and balance relationship to the rubric tolerances.","Render the gold DOCX with a standard office suite and confirm that it remains within two pages.","Walk every rubric item against the rendered document and verify that the conditional recommendation, must/should distinction and fallback are reachable without external sources."]},"design_constraints":{"artifact_scope":"multi_artifact_bundle","structure":"strict_schema"},"reference_files":[],"deliverables":[{"deliverable_id":"corrected_case_response","filename":"cinderhall_pension_case_response.docx","media_type":"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document","description":"Executive-ready corrected pension-access case response for peer approval.","required":true,"constraints":["Place the file at the top level of the working directory.","Use exactly the specified filename.","Limit the rendered document to two pages.","Use England pension and income-tax rules as at 14 April 2025.","Do not invent investment returns, provider charges or provider-specific product features."],"rendering_required":true}],"work_activity":"make_decisions","work_product":"decision_memo","internet_policy":"forbidden","evidence_policy":"closed_world"},"source_metadata":{"prompt_version":"authored_doc@v4","occupation":"personal_financial_advisors","sector":"finance_insurance","task_archetype":"authored_doc","estimated_professional_hours":4.0,"gold_policy_self_report_not_verified":{"author_type":"domain_expert","reviewer_policy":"The reference response is to be reviewed independently by a UK retirement-planning specialist for tax, pension-access and stronger-nudge accuracy, and by a second reviewer for document usability, arithmetic consistency and compliance with the two-page limit.","minimum_reviewers":2,"known_limitations":["Provider-specific benefit classification, access availability and charges are intentionally unresolved.","The projection uses England rates and frozen 2026/27 income-tax assumptions rather than live data.","The response addresses a limited withdrawal objective and is not a complete retirement-income suitability assessment."],"reference_solution_passed_rubric":true}},"validation":{"label":"成品包存在","detail":"按公开题面存在纳入抽样；不视为内容正确性验收。","formal":false,"release_ready_recorded":true,"checks":[{"check":"package_integrity","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T14:54:31+00:00"},{"check":"gold_isolation","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T14:54:31+00:00"},{"check":"artifact_format","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T14:54:31+00:00"},{"check":"rendering","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T14:54:31+00:00"}]},"source_path":"/inspire/hdd2/project/special-project-1/public/ynan/gdpval_data/corpus_main/runs/5d71b90ece963b95","declared_deliverables":["cinderhall_pension_case_response.docx"]};
